1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,420
Chai Corner began as one stall,
with Ravi doing it all alone.

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:07,339
Now it has five outlets and a head office.

3
00:00:07,339 --> 00:00:09,251
But is it actually doing well?

4
00:00:09,801 --> 00:00:11,605
Kabir says, sales are up.

5
00:00:11,605 --> 00:00:12,821
We're doing great.

6
00:00:13,121 --> 00:00:15,197
Asha asks, are the staff happy?

7
00:00:15,197 --> 00:00:17,171
Meena asks, are the customers?

8
00:00:17,471 --> 00:00:19,505
Everyone has a different answer.

9
00:00:19,505 --> 00:00:20,726
So who is right?

10
00:00:21,376 --> 00:00:23,991
Ravi brings his core team to one table.

11
00:00:24,341 --> 00:00:27,379
He needs one fair way to
judge the whole business.

12
00:00:28,029 --> 00:00:30,556
That is organizational effectiveness.

13
00:00:30,856 --> 00:00:35,559
Organizational effectiveness means
how well an organization achieves its

14
00:00:35,559 --> 00:00:36,459
goals.

15
00:00:36,594 --> 00:00:41,387
Think of it as a report card for the
whole organization, not just one number.

16
00:00:42,037 --> 00:00:43,504
Remember episode one?

17
00:00:43,504 --> 00:00:46,041
Effective means doing the right things.

18
00:00:46,391 --> 00:00:49,244
Efficient means doing
things right, with no waste.

19
00:00:49,594 --> 00:00:50,615
You need both.

20
00:00:50,615 --> 00:00:53,454
Doing the wrong thing
very well is still wrong.

21
00:00:54,104 --> 00:00:56,904
There are four ways to
judge effectiveness.

22
00:00:56,904 --> 00:00:58,642
Watch Ravi run all four.

23
00:00:58,992 --> 00:00:59,892
One.

24
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:00,753
The goal approach.

25
00:01:00,753 --> 00:01:02,380
Did we reach our goals?

26
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:05,231
Ravi aimed for five
outlets, and got there.

27
00:01:05,581 --> 00:01:06,481
Two.

28
00:01:06,064 --> 00:01:07,513
The resource approach.

29
00:01:07,513 --> 00:01:12,190
Can we get what we need from outside,
like milk, money and skilled staff?

30
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:13,440
Three.

31
00:01:13,175 --> 00:01:15,248
The internal process approach.

32
00:01:15,248 --> 00:01:18,821
Does work inside run smoothly,
with little conflict?

33
00:01:19,171 --> 00:01:20,071
Four.

34
00:01:19,695 --> 00:01:21,266
The stakeholder approach.

35
00:01:21,266 --> 00:01:23,287
Are the groups we affect happy?

36
00:01:23,287 --> 00:01:26,487
Customers, staff, owners,
suppliers and the town.

37
00:01:27,137 --> 00:01:29,339
Bigger is not the same as better.

38
00:01:30,039 --> 00:01:32,250
Now look back at Ravi's whole journey.

39
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:35,658
He started alone, with one
stall and a long queue.

40
00:01:36,008 --> 00:01:40,804
He built a team, and learned to plan,
organize, staff, direct and control.

41
00:01:41,154 --> 00:01:44,207
He opened the bus stand
outlet, then grew to five.

42
00:01:44,557 --> 00:01:48,210
He learned to decide, to delegate,
and to choose the right span.

43
00:01:48,860 --> 00:01:52,564
At work today, managers keep
score with a few clear signs.

44
00:01:52,914 --> 00:01:55,311
Profit, the money left after costs.

45
00:01:55,311 --> 00:01:57,863
A bakery checks its profit every month.

46
00:01:58,213 --> 00:02:01,228
Productivity, the output
for each hour of work.

47
00:02:01,228 --> 00:02:03,371
A factory counts units per shift.

48
00:02:03,721 --> 00:02:05,208
Customer satisfaction.

49
00:02:05,208 --> 00:02:08,231
A hotel asks every guest
to rate their stay.

50
00:02:08,581 --> 00:02:10,128
Employee satisfaction.

51
00:02:10,128 --> 00:02:12,779
A company runs a yearly staff survey.

52
00:02:13,129 --> 00:02:15,597
Adaptability, changing with the times.

53
00:02:15,597 --> 00:02:19,129
A shop starts selling online
when its buyers move there.

54
00:02:19,779 --> 00:02:21,817
But every scorecard has a trap.

55
00:02:22,167 --> 00:02:26,819
If Ravi only counts cups sold, staff
will rush, and the chai gets worse.

56
00:02:27,169 --> 00:02:31,946
In 1975, the British economist
Charles Goodhart pointed to this trap.

57
00:02:31,946 --> 00:02:36,295
Once a number becomes the target,
it stops telling the truth.

58
00:02:36,645 --> 00:02:38,768
And groups want different things.

59
00:02:38,768 --> 00:02:40,495
Customers want low prices.

60
00:02:40,495 --> 00:02:41,932
Staff want good pay.

61
00:02:42,282 --> 00:02:43,284
So the lesson.

62
00:02:43,284 --> 00:02:46,742
Use many measures, and keep
checking what they really mean.

63
00:02:47,392 --> 00:02:48,348
Quick check.

64
00:02:48,348 --> 00:02:52,194
Ravi asks customers, staff and
suppliers if they are happy.

65
00:02:52,194 --> 00:02:53,811
Which approach is this?

66
00:02:55,711 --> 00:02:57,302
The stakeholder approach!

67
00:02:57,302 --> 00:02:59,971
He checks every group
the business affects.

68
00:03:00,571 --> 00:03:01,584
So, remember.

69
00:03:01,884 --> 00:03:07,075
Organizational effectiveness is how
well an organization achieves its goals.

70
00:03:07,425 --> 00:03:08,976
Judge it four ways.

71
00:03:08,976 --> 00:03:12,650
Goals, resources, internal
process and stakeholders.

72
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,361
Effective means doing the right things.

73
00:03:15,361 --> 00:03:17,391
Efficient means doing them right.

74
00:03:17,991 --> 00:03:19,464
That's the full series.

75
00:03:19,464 --> 00:03:23,940
From one stall to five outlets, you
now know the principles of management.
